      "text": "To find the rightmost three digits of \\(5^{1993}\\), we need to determine \\(5^{1993} \\mod 1000\\). This can be done using modular arithmetic and properties of exponents.\n\nFirst, observe that:\n\\[5^1 = 5\\]\n\\[5^2 = 25\\]\n\\[5^3 = 125\\]\n\\[5^4 = 625\\]\n\\[5^5 = 3125\\]\n\nWe see that for \\(5^n\\) where \\(n \\geq 3\\), the last three digits repeat in a cycle. To confirm, let's find the first few powers of 5 modulo 1000:\n\n\\[5^1 \\equiv 5 \\mod 1000\\]\n\\[5^2 \\equiv 25 \\mod 1000\\]\n\\[5^3 \\equiv 125 \\mod 1000\\]\n\\[5^4 \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000\\]\n\\[5^5 \\equiv 3125 \\equiv 125 \\mod 1000\\]\n\\[5^6 \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000\\]\n\\[5^7 \\equiv 3125 \\equiv 125 \\mod 1000\\]\n\nWe observe that the last three digits of \\(5^n\\) repeat every 2 cycles starting from \\(5^3\\). Therefore, \\(5^{1993} \\mod 1000\\) is the same as \\(5^1 \\mod 1000\\) because 1993 is odd:\n\n\\[5^{1993} \\equiv 5 \\mod 1000\\]\n\nThus, the rightmost three digits of \\(5^{1993}\\) are \\(\\boxed{005}\\).",
