      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the rightmost three digits of \\(5^{1993}\\), we need to determine \\(5^{1993} \\mod 1000\\). We can use the Chinese Remainder Theorem by breaking this down into finding \\(5^{1993} \\mod 8\\) and \\(5^{1993} \\mod 125\\) and then combining the results.\n\nFirst, consider \\(5^{1993} \\mod 8\\):\nSince \\(5 \\equiv 5 \\mod 8\\), we need to find the pattern in the powers of 5 modulo 8. We have:\n\\[\n5^1 \\equiv 5 \\mod 8\n\\]\n\\[\n5^2 \\equiv 25 \\equiv 1 \\mod 8\n\\]\nSo, for any positive integer \\(n\\), \\(5^n \\equiv 1 \\mod 8\\) if \\(n\\) is even, and \\(5^n \\equiv 5 \\mod 8\\) if \\(n\\) is odd. Since 1993 is odd, we have:\n\\[\n5^{1993} \\equiv 5 \\mod 8\n\\]\n\nNext, consider \\(5^{1993} \\mod 125\\):\nSince \\(5^{12} = 2^{24} \\equiv 0 \\mod 125\\), any power of 5 greater than or equal to 12 will be a multiple of 125. Therefore:\n\\[\n5^{1993} \\equiv 0 \\mod 125\n\\]\n\nNow we need to combine these results using the Chinese Remainder Theorem. We need to find a number \\(x\\) such that:\n\\[\nx \\equiv 5 \\mod 8\n\\]\n\\[\nx \\equiv 0 \\mod 125\n\\]\nThis means that \\(x = 125k\\) for some integer \\(k\\). We need \\(125k \\equiv 5 \\mod 8\\). Since \\(125 \\equiv 5 \\mod 8\\), we have:\n\\[\n5k \\equiv 5 \\mod 8\n\\]\nDividing both sides by 5 (noting that 5 and 8 are coprime, so division is valid):\n\\[\nk \\equiv 1 \\mod 8\n\\]\nSo, \\(k = 8m + 1\\) for some integer \\(m\\), and thus:\n\\[\nx = 125k = 125(8m + 1) = 1000m + 125\n\\]\nTherefore, \\(x \\equiv 125 \\mod 1000\\). So, the rightmost three digits of \\(5^{1993}\\) are 125.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{125}\n\\]",
